  "headline": "NVIDIA consensus rises to $376bn; our $374.62bn base is now $1.38bn below street",
  "note": "Fictional Securities raises its own calendar-2026 NVIDIA Data Center estimate from $370bn to $385bn and reports compiled consensus rising from $372bn to $376bn; AMD GPU consensus stays $14bn [broker-oct2026]. The stated period, revenue metric and dollar-billion units match our benchmark. Current view already adopts $376bn. The legacy “2024” label is stale; the October 5 snapshot and new note explicitly say 2026 [street-oct2026]. Tracker membership, weighting and sampling cutoff remain undisclosed.\n\nI partly agree with the broker’s capex direction: our $682.5bn estimate already exceeds street by $15bn. Its note supplies no company estimates, measures, accelerator mix, supplier split, concentration or quarterly bridge. Our Meta and Alphabet estimates sit at disclosed guide midpoints, so “above guidance midpoints” is not established across all four [meta-2026-07-29-release] [googl-2026-02-04-release].\n\nHolding our drivers, $385bn requires $701.42bn capex, $18.92bn above our estimate. Alternatively, with our Q3 $103bn held, it requires Q4 $145.44bn versus our own $128bn. These are reverse-engineered sensitivities, not disclosed broker assumptions. The $376bn tracker requires Q4 $131.94bn on the same convention.\n\nI retain every input and the Q3 forecast; late-October capex and November supplier guidance will test the gap. NVIDIA’s long relative earnings case weakens because the street moved: +$2.62bn becomes -$1.38bn (-0.37%); our forecast is unchanged.",
  "stance": "Capex remains above street in our model; NVIDIA is now modestly below consensus as custom silicon and timing constrain capture.",
  "rationale": "The new tracker explicitly uses calendar 2026 NVIDIA Data Center revenue and is comparable on stated scope to the prior snapshot. The application already tracks $376bn; its compilation methodology is not independently auditable from this short fictional note. The broker’s only stated driver is capex above guide midpoints, without quantified company or supplier assumptions. Our unchanged forecast becomes modestly below street because the benchmark rose $4bn. No new company guidance or delivery evidence warrants changing capex, drivers or quarterly estimates.",
  "thesis": "Calendar 2026 capex remains $682.5bn versus the unchanged $667.5bn October 5 capex street. NVIDIA Data Center bear/base/bull remains $351.34bn/$374.62bn/$391.79bn versus updated $376bn consensus, giving gaps -$24.66bn/-$1.38bn/+$15.79bn. AMD GPU base stays $14.00bn versus unchanged $14bn. The base NVIDIA gap moved from +0.70% to -0.37% entirely through the benchmark. Broker house $385bn is $10.38bn above our base, within our bull range, but its actual company capex and capture assumptions remain undisclosed. Base mix/split/concentration stay 38%/65%/45%; bull NVIDIA share stays 65.5%. NVIDIA Q3 FY27 Data Center remains $103bn, expected November 18 unconfirmed, and own Q4 $128bn versus $129.86bn required by our annual base.",
